+# GEMM bias epilogue fusion on BI-V150
+
+A vendor Triton 2.1.0 `tl.dot` GEMM passed FP16/BF16 checks for 127×65×33, 256×256×256, and 512×512×512. Four BM/BN/BK/warp settings were tested in three independent processes per dtype and shape. The fused store applied a column bias before writing the final output; the paired baseline used the **same GEMM tile** followed by `torch.add(out=)`. The selected candidate for each shape passed against a CPU FP64 reference and the paired baseline in every round, and was faster in each round.
+
+The central epilogue code in the [retained runnable source](../../evidence/bi-v150-corex42-migration-batch1/stage4_gemm_epilogue.py) is:
+
+```python
+result = accumulator.to(C.dtype.element_ty)
+bias = tl.load(BIAS + cols, mask=cols < N, other=0)
+result = (result.to(tl.float32) + bias.to(tl.float32)).to(C.dtype.element_ty)
+tl.store(C + rows[:, None] * N + cols[None, :], result,
+         mask=(rows[:, None] < M) & (cols[None, :] < N))
+```
+
+The full source also includes `tl.dot`, masks, launch grid, correctness, and paired GPU-event timing. For 127 and 256 cases, 32×64×32 with 8 warps had the lowest median fused time among the tested candidates; for 512 it was 64×64×32 with 4 warps. This is a **shape-specific shortlist**, not a general tile recommendation. The 1.334–3.936× gains include removing a PyTorch bias launch and do not compare against a vendor optimized GEMM or measure end-to-end model latency. The [source record](../../sources/experiments/bi-v150-corex42-migration-batch1.md) hashes all raw runs and logs.
