+# Translating Blackwell kernel ideas to BI-V150
+
+Keep the original SM90/SM100 pages as architecture-specific references. A tiling or fusion idea is a hypothesis for BI-V150 until the corresponding implementation compiles, passes the target's numerical contract, and is measured against an appropriate target baseline.
+
+| Blackwell/Hopper reference | BI-V150 action |
+|---|---|
+| Triton index, mask, and `tl.dot` structure | Start from a small vendor-backend kernel; FP32 add and FP16 GEMM passed in the retained CoreX 4.2 run. |
+| Tile sizes, warp counts, and stage counts | Search anew with fixed shapes and correctness checks. Stage-0 timings are too short to recommend a configuration. |
+| `wgmma`, `tcgen05`, TMEM, TMA, CLC, NVIDIA PTX | Do not copy instruction or synchronization claims. A corresponding BI-V150 capability has not been established here. |
+| Precision and accumulation choices | Recheck target dtype support and error tolerance; FP32 add, FP16/BF16 GEMM, and FP16/BF16 forward RMSNorm passed the retained smoke checks on this Pod. |
+| Performance numbers on H100/B200 | Keep GPU, dtype, shape, metric, and source attached; never relabel them as BI-V150 results. |
+
+The [BI-V150 Triton page](../languages/triton-iluvatar.md) contains the observed JIT compatibility fix. Before promoting a migrated technique, save the exact CoreX/Triton version, device, source hash, failing candidates, numerical checks, and repeated timings. A newer FlagTree 3.1 backend is documented separately from the tested Triton 2.1.0 build.
+
+## Priority translation paths
+
+| NVIDIA knowledge | Portable hypothesis | BI-V150 status | Next evidence needed |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| [Kernel fusion](../techniques/kernel-fusion.md) | Fuse residual add and RMSNorm, then reduce weight-gradient partials without target-end atomics | [Forward](../kernels/residual-rmsnorm-bi-v150.md) passed FP16/BF16 checks on five shapes; the separate [two-kernel backward](../kernels/residual-rmsnorm-backward-bi-v150.md) passed 72 case-rounds across six shapes and both observable-gradient modes. Their synthetic autograd composition passed 18 case-rounds with a weight update. Forward and backward timings have distinct PyTorch baselines | Compare the backward with another optimized target implementation and measure a real training step before drawing throughput conclusions |
+| [Epilogue fusion](../techniques/epilogue-fusion.md) | Apply scaling or an elementwise operation before a result is written | [FP16/BF16 GEMM+bias](../kernels/gemm-bias-bi-v150.md) passed three-process target checks and same-tile paired timing; the benefit includes removal of a PyTorch bias launch | Compare with vendor-optimized GEMM and model-level calls |
+| [Tile scheduling](../techniques/tile-scheduling.md) | Search tile and warp settings for a fixed workload | Four target configurations were compared on three GEMM shapes; the best candidate changed at 512×512×512 | Expand shape/layout coverage and inspect compiled resources |
+
+These rows describe migration work, not a claim that an NVIDIA kernel or its
+speedup runs on BI-V150. The [technique-by-technique migration ledger](../../references/iluvatar-migration-ledger.md)
+records the remaining experiments; only verified target behavior is promoted
+into this skill's capability pages.
+
+## Additional target results and limits
+
+The [affine access case](../kernels/affine-access-bi-v150.md) records contiguous and stride-2 timing alongside `n_regs`/`n_spills`; it does not prove a particular vector load instruction. The [two-pass scan](../kernels/scan-bi-v150.md) is correct on two tail shapes but slower than `torch.cumsum`. In the same CoreX 4.2 batch, `num_stages=1/2/3` and `tl.load` cache modifiers compiled and ran, yet no NVIDIA pipeline/cache equivalence was established. An FP8 tensor allocation succeeded while FP8 `tl.dot` failed to compile on the tested vendor build. A fixed-grid persistent add was correct but slower and did not use CLC. The original SM90/SM100 technique and hardware pages remain architecture-scoped; see the [full migration ledger](../../references/iluvatar-migration-ledger.md) and [raw source](../../sources/experiments/bi-v150-corex42-migration-batch1.md) before reusing any result.
+
+The [scheduling audit](../../sources/experiments/bi-v150-corex42-scheduling-audit.md) adds compiler IR evidence: stage 2/3 GEMM uses `insert_slice_async`/commit/wait in TTGIR and 2/3 shared-memory buffers, but the tested 256³ case was slower than stage 1. A `tl.swizzle2d` tile-order experiment was correct with no robust speedup; it does not probe shared-memory bank swizzling. A dual-accumulator K loop was correct but slower at 512³ and does not reproduce SM100 TMEM ping-pong. Setting `enable_warp_specialization=True` left one target GEMM's TTGIR/LLVM IR identical to default. Do not infer a hardware-equivalent warp role split from an accepted option.
+
+The same 32×32×32 FP16 GEMM+bias tile was replicated on 512×512×2048 and 1024³ workloads in three independent processes. All stages passed numerical checks; stage 2 versus stage 1 gave 1.0154× and 1.0018×, while stage 3 gave 0.7724× and 0.9166×. This establishes a workload-dependent scheduling result, not a generic rule to increase stage count. See the scheduling audit's Stage 10 receipts and the full migration ledger.
+
+A subsequent three-process holdout added BF16 and a 64×64×32 tile. All 72 numerical checks passed. Stage 2 on BF16 512×512×2048 with the 32 tile gave 1.0162× versus stage 1, but on BF16 1024³ with the 64 tile it gave 0.9152×; stage 3 on the latter gave 0.7484×. Choose stage count jointly with dtype, shape, and tile using target measurements. The [scheduling audit](../../sources/experiments/bi-v150-corex42-scheduling-audit.md) retains all raw samples and compiler metadata.
